  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Fifty stone terraces vanished in a single night. Lake Rotomahana, New Zealand, the tenth of June 1886. Mount Tarawera tore open a seventeen-kilometre rift.",
        "visual_query": "mount tarawera volcanic rift crater",
        "visual_fallback": "volcanic crater ridge"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Before that, the Pink and White Terraces were the country's great attraction. Silica staircases, dropping twenty-five metres over fifty basins of warm water.",
        "visual_query": "silica terrace hot spring pools",
        "visual_fallback": "steaming thermal pools"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Victorians sailed the world to bathe there. Then the crater filled and the lake rose thirty to forty metres. Where exactly had the terraces been?",
        "visual_query": "new zealand geothermal lake steam",
        "visual_fallback": "steaming lake shore"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2010 an archivist in Basel opened Ferdinand von Hochstetter's 1859 survey diary. His bearings were the last accurate fix on the lost shoreline.",
        "visual_query": "old survey diary map archive",
        "visual_fallback": "antique survey map"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Two teams read it differently: buried under the shore, or drowned by the new lake. The latest reconstruction, diary plus lidar, puts them underwater.",
        "visual_query": "lake rotomahana shoreline aerial",
        "visual_fallback": "lake shoreline aerial"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
    "word_count": 120,
